# Bounce for every Coder app: path apps, subdomain apps and ports

**Status:** findings measured on Coder v2.36.5 (local lab, September 2026) and confirmed by hand in Chrome. The generalised design below reuses the validated plane-host bounce (`plane-host-cookie-bounce.md`). Its new parts (purpose `app`, the app-only key with rotation, `cookie_path` in the Lambda, ttyd as the terminal) are **implemented and validated on AWS** (September 27, 2026): 40 headless checks plus a manual browser test with VS Code and ttyd (section 3.9). Lab scripts: `spike-middle/aws-apps-bounce-lab/`. **Subdomain apps and ports** (the bounce runs on the app host and sets Coder's own subdomain app cookie) are **implemented and validated in the local lab** (33 headless checks, section 3.10); not yet on AWS. **The bounce as a Go microservice** (a Deployment proving itself with a projected service-account token, instead of a Lambda): the bouncer is built and unit-tested, and the middle's side is tested sample code (section 9). **Scopes for CLI and IDE tokens** measured in a local lab (44 headless checks plus a manual test with VS Code Desktop and interactive `coder ssh`, section 10).

**Related:** [plane-host-cookie-bounce.md](./plane-host-cookie-bounce.md) (the bounce: ALB `/_portal/*` → Lambda → middle redeem), [middle-go-launch-link.md](./middle-go-launch-link.md) (one link `/go/<slug>`; section 7 = bounce hand-off, section 7.4 = keeping users out of the dashboard, section 8 = JSON + token strip), [open-coder-apps-portal-json-strip.md](./open-coder-apps-portal-json-strip.md) (the token strip; works only for users signed in to the dashboard, 1.1).

---

## TL;DR

- **A token in the URL (`?coder_session_token=`) gives an app only 60 seconds, path or subdomain.** Coder turns the token into a 60-second app cookie and nothing else; it never renews it. After that, a path app gets Coder's login page on the next reload or reconnect, and a subdomain app is sent to the dashboard's auth redirect, which only works for a user signed in to the dashboard (the login page otherwise). So JSON + token strip does not work for users kept out of the dashboard (1.1).
- **A leftover `coder_session_token` cookie beats the token in the URL.** Coder reads the cookie first; a stale one sends the user to the login page even with a valid token in the URL.
- **Decision: open every Coder page through the bounce.** The frontend always does the same thing: ask the middle for a one-time code and form-POST it to the `bounce_url` the middle returns (`/_portal/bounce` on the plane host, or on the app's hostname for subdomain apps and ports). The middle decides per target which key, cookie name, domain, path, lifetime and redirect to use. The frontend never has to know whether an app is a path or a subdomain app. The Lambda does the same thing for every purpose; OAuth consent differs from apps only by its one-off `/_portal/done` step (revoke the key and clear the cookie), and apps get an app-only key in a narrowly scoped cookie (section 3.2).
- **The terminal becomes an ordinary app: ttyd as a path app, Coder's built-in web terminal hidden** (section 3.7). The user's full Coder key then never reaches the browser.
- **The bounce can also keep users out of the Coder dashboard.** For apps, the cookie holds an app-only key (`application_connect`) and is limited to the workspace's apps path. Validated on AWS with VS Code and ttyd: both work past 60 s and after a reload, websockets included, and the Coder root shows the login page (section 3.9).
- **Subdomain apps and ports use the same bounce, run on the app's own hostname.** The load balancer also sends `/_portal/*` on the wildcard hostnames to the Lambda. It sets Coder's subdomain app cookie (`coder_subdomain_app_session_token_<hash>`, `Domain=` the wildcard parent, like Coder does) holding the same app-only key. One bounce then covers every subdomain app and port of the user on that plane, and the dashboard ignores the cookie. Validated in the local lab (section 3.10).
- **The bounce does not have to be a Lambda.** The same Go code runs as a small Deployment next to Coder (`cmd/bounce-server`). The ALB on EKS, or NGINX or the router on OpenShift, sends `/_portal/*` to it. It proves itself to the middle with a projected service-account token, which the middle verifies offline through the EKS cluster's public OIDC discovery and JWKS (section 9).
- **CLI and IDE tokens: `coder:workspaces.access` + `user:read`, not the default `coder:all`.** Add `coder:workspaces.operate` if users should start and stop workspaces from the CLI. Without `user:read`, `/users/me` answers 404, so `coder login` and the VS Code Desktop Coder extension reject the token (validated by hand). With it, the token can SSH, port-forward and connect, but cannot mint tokens or create workspaces (section 10).

