## 2. Decision

**Every Coder page the portal (or a `/go/` link) opens goes through the plane-host bounce.** The browser only ever carries a one-time code in a POST body. The Coder cookie is set by a response from the Coder hostname (the Lambda behind the ALB rule `/_portal/*`).

| Target | Token in the URL (JSON + strip) | Bounce |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Path app (`subdomain = false`) | **dead after 60 s** (1.1); a stale cookie blocks it (1.2) | works; app-only key + path cookie keeps the dashboard closed (1.3) |
| Subdomain app or port | **dead after 60 s** unless the user is signed in to the dashboard (1.1) | works: bounce on the app hostname, app-only key in Coder's subdomain app cookie (3.10, validated locally) |
| Terminal: ttyd as a path app (replaces Coder's web terminal, 3.7) | dead after 60 s, like any path app | works like any path app (app-only key + path cookie) |
| External-auth consent (GitLab) | does not work (cookie-only Coder page) | works (validated on AWS) |

Why the bounce for everything:

- **One mechanism.** Templates can mix path and subdomain apps; the frontend should not care. The middle knows each app's `subdomain` flag and picks the right redirect.
- **No token in any URL, log, history or frontend JavaScript.**
- **Stale cookies cannot break it** (it overwrites them).
- **The same infrastructure** (ALB rule, Lambda, IAM trust) already exists for the web terminal and GitLab consent. Subdomain apps and ports add one ALB rule for the wildcard hostnames (3.10).

**Terminal: ttyd instead of Coder's web terminal.** Coder's web terminal is a page of the dashboard: it needs the user's full key in a `Path=/` cookie, which also opens the dashboard. ttyd, run in the workspace as a normal path app, is opened like VS Code or JupyterLab, so the full key never reaches the browser (3.7).

The token URL is no fallback for users kept out of the dashboard: it lasts 60 s for every kind of app (1.1).

