## 3. General design

### 3.1 One entry point; the middle decides

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant B as Browser (portal or /go/ page)
  participant M as Middle
  participant L as Bounce Lambda (coder-a.example.com/_portal/*)
  participant C as Coder (coder-a.example.com)

  B->>M: POST /v1/bounce/start {plane, purpose, workspace_id?, slug?, provider?}
  M->>C: read workspace and app (user's full key, server side)
  M->>M: pick key kind, cookie path, redirect; issue one-time code (60 s)
  M-->>B: {bounce_url, code}
  B->>L: form POST code
  L->>M: POST /internal/bounce/redeem (signed STS proof)
  M-->>L: {session_token, redirect_path, cookie_name, cookie_domain, cookie_path, max_age}
  L-->>B: 302 redirect_path + Set-Cookie (HttpOnly, Secure)
  B->>C: the app / terminal / consent page
```

`bounce_url` is the plane host for path apps, the terminal and consent, and the app's own hostname for subdomain apps and ports (3.10). The Lambda behind it is the same.

The portal already has the frontend half: `openViaBounce(body)` in `portal/public/app.js` asks for a code and form-POSTs it. Nothing changes there. Only the body differs:

```javascript
openViaBounce({ plane, purpose: "app", workspace_id: wsId, slug: "code-server" }); // new
openViaBounce({ plane, purpose: "app", workspace_id: wsId, slug: "jupyter" });     // new
openViaBounce({ plane, purpose: "app", workspace_id: wsId, slug: "ttyd" });        // new: the terminal
openViaBounce({ plane, purpose: "port", workspace_id: wsId, port: 8000 });         // new: a port (subdomain)
openViaBounce({ plane, purpose: "external-auth", provider: "primary-gitlab" }, { sameTab: true }); // existing
```

The `/go/<slug>` link (`middle-go-launch-link.md` section 7) uses the same code issuance through its `Handoff` hook. There the middle renders the form itself, so there is no frontend code at all.

### 3.2 What the middle picks per target

**The Lambda does the same thing for every purpose.** It takes the code, redeems it with the middle, sets the cookie from the reply, and redirects. The differences between OAuth consent and an app are all decided by the middle: which key it mints, its lifetime, the cookie name, domain and path, and the redirect. They reach the Lambda as data in the redeem reply, so the Lambda never branches on the purpose; it only checks them against fixed rules (3.5).

OAuth consent and apps differ in two ways only:

| | OAuth consent (`external-auth`) | Coder app (`app`) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Lifecycle | one-off: after the consent, Coder returns to `/_portal/done`, where the Lambda asks the middle to revoke the key, clears the cookie and sends the browser back to the portal | ongoing: the cookie must stay valid while the app is used. There is no `/done` step, and none is needed, because the key is app-only and cannot open the dashboard |
| Cookie | dedicated 5-minute key, `Path=/` (the consent pages live under several paths) | reused app-only key, up to 8 h: on the workspace's apps path (path apps), or in Coder's subdomain app cookie (subdomain apps and ports) |

The `/_portal/done` step already exists and was validated on AWS (`plane-host-cookie-bounce.md`). Apps add the cookie name, domain and path to the redeem reply.

Per target:

| Purpose | Key in the cookie | Cookie path | Cookie lifetime | Redirect after the bounce | Afterwards | Status |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `app`, path app | `middle-app-<plane>`: app-only (`application_connect`), cached per user and plane, shared by all apps and workspaces | the workspace's apps prefix, e.g. `/@alice/notebook/apps` (one cookie per workspace) | up to 8 h, never beyond the key's remaining lifetime | `/@alice/notebook/apps/code-server/` | nothing | implemented, validated on AWS (3.9) |
| `app`, subdomain app; `port` | the same `middle-app-<plane>` key, in Coder's subdomain app cookie `coder_subdomain_app_session_token_<hash>`, `Domain=` the wildcard parent | `/` | up to 8 h, never beyond the key's remaining lifetime | `/` on the app hostname (the bounce runs there) | nothing | implemented, validated locally (3.10) |
| `terminal` (Coder's web terminal) | the user's full `middle-<plane>` key | `/` | 900 s | `/@alice/notebook.main/terminal` | the dashboard is reachable with it | validated, **retired**: use ttyd as an `app` (3.7) |
| `external-auth` | `bounce-<plane>`: dedicated, 5 min | `/` | 300 s | `/external-auth/<id>/callback?redirect=/_portal/done` | key revoked and cookie cleared at `/_portal/done` | validated on AWS |

For subdomain apps we first considered going through Coder's own auth redirect (bounce a short-lived key onto `/api/v2/applications/auth-redirect` and let Coder mint the app cookie). The lab ruled it out: that endpoint only accepts a full key (1.1), so the browser would briefly hold a key that opens the dashboard, and the resulting cookie expires with that key. Setting Coder's subdomain cookie directly, with the app-only key, needs neither (3.10).

### 3.3 Middle: `POST /v1/bounce/start`, purpose `app`

A new case in `handleBounceStart` (`go-middle/bounce.go`; implemented as `appBounceTarget`). The redirect and the cookie path are computed from Coder's data, never taken from the request. The implementation also checks that the workspace belongs to the caller (apps are `share = "owner"`), that it is running (409 otherwise), and maps Coder's 403/404 on the workspace to a 404. The sketch:

```go
	case "app":
		if !appSlugRe.MatchString(in.Slug) { // ^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{0,63}$
			http.Error(w, "invalid app slug", http.StatusBadRequest)
			return
		}
		tok, err := s.ensureCoderTokenOnPlane(r.Context(), email, p.ID) // full key, server side only
		if err != nil {
			http.Error(w, err.Error(), http.StatusBadGateway)
			return
		}
		raw, err := p.Client.WorkspaceRaw(r.Context(), tok, in.WorkspaceID)
		if err != nil {
			http.Error(w, err.Error(), http.StatusBadGateway)
			return
		}
		me, err := p.Client.GetMe(r.Context(), tok)
		if err != nil {
			http.Error(w, err.Error(), http.StatusBadGateway)
			return
		}
		app, agent := findApp(raw, in.Slug) // latest build → resources → agents → apps
		switch {
		case app == nil:
			http.Error(w, "no such app in this workspace", http.StatusNotFound)
			return
		case app.Health != "healthy" && app.Health != "disabled":
			http.Error(w, "app is starting", http.StatusConflict)
			return
		case app.Subdomain:
			return s.subdomainTarget(p, app.SubdomainName) // bounce on the app host, 3.10
		}
		name, _ := raw["name"].(string)
		wsPart := url.PathEscape(name)
		if agentCount(raw) > 1 {
			wsPart += "." + url.PathEscape(agent) // multi-agent workspaces: /@user/ws.agent/apps/slug/
		}
		appsPrefix := fmt.Sprintf("/@%s/%s/apps", url.PathEscape(me.Username), wsPart)
		redirect = appsPrefix + "/" + url.PathEscape(in.Slug) + "/"
		cookiePath = appsPrefix // one cookie covers every app of this workspace (same app-only key)
```

The request body gets `slug` (and `port`, `protocol` for purpose `port`). The code record carries the whole target, including the one host allowed to redeem it:

```go
type bounceCode struct {
	Email, Plane, Purpose string
	Host                  string // the only host allowed to redeem (plane host, or the app host)
	Target                bounceTarget
	Exp                   time.Time
}

type bounceTarget struct {
	Origin       string // scheme://host[:port] serving the bounce
	Redirect     string // relative, on Origin
	CookieName   string // "" = coder_session_token
	CookieDomain string // "" = host-only
	CookiePath   string // "" = "/"
}
```

### 3.4 Middle: redeem

In `handleBounceRedeem`, `app` and `port` get the app-only key, and the reply carries the cookie attributes from the code's target (`cookie_name`, `cookie_domain`, `cookie_path`; simplified below to the path). The request's `Host` must equal the code's `Host`: a code issued for one app host cannot set a cookie from another app host or from the plane host (validated, 3.10).

```go
	cookiePath := ""
	switch rec.Purpose {
	case "external-auth":
		maxAge = bounceConsentTTLSeconds
		tok, err = s.mintBounceToken(r.Context(), rec.Email, p, time.Duration(maxAge)*time.Second)
	case "app", "port":
		var left time.Duration
		tok, left, err = s.ensureAppTokenOnPlane(r.Context(), rec.Email, p)
		maxAge = min(s.Config.Bounce.AppCookieMaxAgeSeconds, int(left.Seconds()))
		cookiePath = rec.Target.CookiePath
	default: // terminal (Coder's web terminal; retired in favour of ttyd as an app, 3.7)
		tok, err = s.ensureCoderTokenOnPlane(r.Context(), rec.Email, p.ID)
	}
	// ...
	writeJSON(w, http.StatusOK, map[string]any{
		"session_token": tok,
		"redirect_path": rec.Redirect,
		"cookie_path":   cookiePath,
		"max_age":       maxAge,
	})
```

`ensureAppTokenOnPlane` keeps one current app-only key per user and plane, cached with its expiry. Each key gets a unique name, so a new one never has to replace the old one:

```go
	body := map[string]any{
		"token_name": fmt.Sprintf("middle-app-%s-%s", p.ID, time.Now().UTC().Format("060102150405")), // unique per mint
		"lifetime":   int64(24 * time.Hour), // app_key_lifetime_seconds
		"scope":      "application_connect", // stored as scopes ["coder:application_connect"]
	}
```

On a cache hit the middle also checks, with the owner token, that the key still exists in Coder. If an admin deleted it, the bounce mints a new one instead of handing out a dead cookie.

**Rotation without deleting.**

| Key's remaining lifetime | What the bounce does | Cookie `Max-Age` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| more than `app_cookie_max_age_seconds` (8 h) | reuse the cached key | 8 h |
| less than that | mint a new key (new name) and cache it; **leave the old one alone** | 8 h (the new key has 24 h) |

A cookie therefore never outlives its key. Tabs still holding an older cookie keep working until that cookie ends, because its key is still valid. Deleting the old key on rotation (the delete-then-create pattern the main key uses) would log those tabs out. Minting on every launch would pile up keys for nothing.

**Coder cleans up expired keys itself.** `coderd` purges keys that have been expired for longer than `CODER_API_KEYS_RETENTION` (default `7d`; `0` disables purging). The job runs every 10 minutes and never touches valid keys ([Data retention](https://coder.com/docs/admin/setup/data-retention)). The middle never deletes app keys. Per user and plane there are at most two valid app keys (current and previous) plus the expired ones from the last 7 days waiting for the purge.

Keep the name short, because Coder validates token names. With a short plane ID, `middle-app-a-260927204629` is 25 characters.

**Where the cache lives.** In memory, a middle restart forgets the key, and the next bounce mints a new one. That is harmless now that nothing is deleted, but it adds keys. With several middle replicas, each one would mint its own key. For production, use a shared store (Redis) with a time-to-live equal to the key's remaining lifetime, and encrypt the value, since it is a credential.

Config (`BounceConfig`): `app_cookie_max_age_seconds`, default `28800` (8 h), and `app_key_lifetime_seconds`, default `86400` (24 h). The middle refuses to start unless the key lifetime is longer than the cookie lifetime; keep it well above, so a rotated key still covers the cookies issued just before the rotation.

### 3.5 Lambda: cookie name, domain and path

The Lambda (`bounce-lambda/bounce/handler.go`) takes the cookie attributes from the middle but only within fixed rules:

| Attribute | Accepted |
| --- | --- |
| `cookie_name` | empty or `coder_session_token`, or `coder_subdomain_app_session_token_` + 32 lowercase hex (`safeCookieName`) |
| `cookie_domain` | empty (host-only), or a **strict parent of the host the request came in on**, and **never** for `coder_session_token` (`safeCookieDomain`). So the dashboard's session cookie always stays host-only |
| `cookie_path` | a safe prefix of the redirect (`safeCookiePath`, below) |
| `redirect_path` | a relative path (`/…`, not `//…`), as before; subdomain apps redirect to `/` on the app host the bounce runs on |

The path check:

```go
	var out struct {
		SessionToken string `json:"session_token"`
		RedirectPath string `json:"redirect_path"`
		CookieName   string `json:"cookie_name"`
		CookieDomain string `json:"cookie_domain"`
		CookiePath   string `json:"cookie_path"`
		MaxAge       int    `json:"max_age"`
	}
	if err := json.NewDecoder(res.Body).Decode(&out); err != nil || out.SessionToken == "" ||
		!strings.HasPrefix(out.RedirectPath, "/") || strings.HasPrefix(out.RedirectPath, "//") {
		return page(http.StatusBadGateway, "Unexpected middle response."), nil
	}
	cookiePath := "/"
	if out.CookiePath != "" {
		if !strings.HasPrefix(out.CookiePath, "/") || strings.HasPrefix(out.CookiePath, "//") ||
			strings.ContainsAny(out.CookiePath, ";, \t\r\n") ||
			!(strings.HasPrefix(out.RedirectPath, out.CookiePath+"/") || strings.HasPrefix(out.RedirectPath, out.CookiePath+"?")) {
			return page(http.StatusBadGateway, "Unexpected middle response."), nil
		}
		cookiePath = out.CookiePath
	}
	cookieName, ok := safeCookieName(out.CookieName)                   // allowlist, table above
	cookieDomain, ok2 := safeCookieDomain(out.CookieDomain, host, cookieName) // strict parent of this host
	if !ok || !ok2 {
		return page(http.StatusBadGateway, "Unexpected middle response."), nil
	}
	cookie := (&http.Cookie{
		Name:     cookieName,
		Value:    out.SessionToken,
		Domain:   cookieDomain,
		Path:     cookiePath,
		MaxAge:   out.MaxAge,
		HttpOnly: true,
		Secure:   h.CookieSecure,
		SameSite: http.SameSiteLaxMode,
	}).String()
```

(The `?` case covers a redirect with a query string on the cookie path itself.)

A cookie path without a trailing slash, `/@alice/notebook/apps`, is sent for every app of that workspace and everything under them, but not for `/@alice/notebook/apps-old` or another workspace (standard browser path matching).

### 3.6 Frontend

Nothing new: `openViaBounce` (3.1). Every launch is the same call with a different `purpose`. The response is always `{bounce_url, code}`; the frontend never sees a Coder token or builds a Coder URL.

To know which buttons to open that way, the portal reads `GET /v1/workspaces/{id}/access`: the middle marks each path app with `"bounce": true` when the plane has a bounce configured, and each subdomain app too when the plane also has `bounce.subdomain_apps: true` and a `wildcard_url`. The portal then calls `openViaBounce({ plane, purpose: "app", workspace_id, slug })` (`runChoice` in `portal/public/app.js`), whatever the kind of app. With `"port_bounce": true` in the same response, the portal shows a port field and an "Open port" button, which calls `openViaBounce({ plane, purpose: "port", workspace_id, port })`.

### 3.7 Terminal: ttyd as a path app

Coder's built-in web terminal cannot be opened with an app-only key. It is a page of the Coder dashboard, served at `/@alice/notebook.main/terminal`, and it calls `/api/v2/workspaceagents/<id>/pty`. So its cookie has to hold the user's full key with `Path=/`, and that cookie also opens the dashboard. Instead, the workspace runs **ttyd** as an ordinary path app, and the built-in terminal is hidden:

```hcl
resource "coder_agent" "main" {
  # ...
  display_apps {
    web_terminal = false # hide Coder's built-in terminal; ttyd below replaces it
    vscode       = false
    # ...
  }

  startup_script = <<-EOT
    # ... other apps ...
    if [ ! -x "$HOME/bin/ttyd" ]; then
      mkdir -p "$HOME/bin"
      case "$(uname -m)" in
        aarch64|arm64) arch=aarch64 ;;
        *)             arch=x86_64 ;;
      esac
      curl -fsSL -o "$HOME/bin/ttyd" "https://github.com/tsl0922/ttyd/releases/download/1.7.7/ttyd.$arch"
      chmod +x "$HOME/bin/ttyd"
    fi
    # -W: writable; 127.0.0.1: only Coder's agent reaches it. Coder authenticates every request.
    nohup "$HOME/bin/ttyd" -i 127.0.0.1 -p 7681 -W bash >/tmp/ttyd.log 2>&1 &
  EOT
}

resource "coder_app" "ttyd" {
  agent_id     = coder_agent.main.id
  slug         = "ttyd"
  display_name = "Terminal"
  url          = "http://127.0.0.1:7681"
  icon         = "/icon/terminal.svg"
  subdomain    = false
  share        = "owner"

  healthcheck {
    url       = "http://127.0.0.1:7681/"
    interval  = 5
    threshold = 30
  }
}
```

In production, bake the ttyd binary into the workspace image instead of downloading it at start (pinned version, no outbound download needed).

The portal opens it like any other app: `openViaBounce({ plane, purpose: "app", workspace_id, slug: "ttyd" })`. The middle's `terminal` purpose and the portal's `openWebTerminal` are then no longer needed.

What changes:

| | Coder's web terminal (before) | ttyd as a path app |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Cookie | full key, `Path=/`, 900 s | app-only key, the workspace's apps prefix `/@alice/notebook/apps`, up to 8 h |
| Dashboard reachable with the cookie | yes | no |
| Opened by | purpose `terminal` | purpose `app`, slug `ttyd` |
| Hidden in the Coder UI | — | `display_apps { web_terminal = false }` |

Hiding the built-in terminal only removes the button. Its URL still works for someone who holds a full key, but with this design the browser never gets one.

**One key per user, one cookie per workspace (the default).** A cookie has one `Path`; the browser keeps cookies with the same name but different paths as separate cookies. The middle therefore sets the cookie on the workspace's apps prefix, `/@alice/notebook/apps` (or `/@alice/notebook.main/apps` for multi-agent workspaces). The first app opened sets it, and it covers ttyd, VS Code, JupyterLab and any app added to the template later. Another workspace gets its own cookie, holding the same `middle-app-<plane>` key. There is no key per app or per workspace, and the prefix still passes the Lambda's check that the cookie path is a prefix of the redirect.

A cookie per app (`/@alice/notebook/apps/code-server`) also works, but only adds cookies. A cookie per user (`Path=/@alice`) would be safe too, because the key is app-only and the dashboard's API lives under `/api/v2`, where the cookie is not sent. The per-workspace prefix is the middle ground: it reaches no more than it needs to, and the middle knows the workspace anyway when it issues the code.

### 3.8 Always bounce on "open"; nobody tracks the cookie

Neither the middle nor the portal can tell whether the cookie is already set. It is HttpOnly and lives on the Coder hostname, so the middle (another hostname) never receives it, and page JavaScript cannot read it. Tracking it in the middle ("issued at 10:02 for 8 h") would be unreliable: the user may clear cookies, switch browser or device, or the key may have been rotated since.

So **every "open" from the portal goes through the bounce**, whether or not a cookie exists:

- **Cheap:** the app-only key is cached, so the redeem mints nothing; the Lambda sets the same cookie again. The cost is one redirect plus the middle's checks.
- **Self-healing:** a missing, expired or stale cookie is replaced (1.2 shows what a stale one does otherwise).
- **It extends the session:** each open resets the cookie's `Max-Age`.

Inside the app nothing bounces: requests, websockets and reloads use the cookie already set. The one case the bounce does not cover is a tab left open longer than the cookie lifetime (`app_cookie_max_age_seconds`, 8 h by default). Its next request gets Coder's login page, and the user opens the app again from the portal. Setting the cookie lifetime close to the key's (for example 20 h for a 24 h key) makes this rarer.

