Coder hides expired keys from the token list right away (a 3-minute test key was gone from the list after it expired); the rows are removed later by the purge (3.4).

**Browser test (Chrome, incognito):** portal → alice → workspace `apps` shows VS Code and Terminal, and no "Web Terminal (Coder)". Both opened in a new tab with no token in the URL. Saving in VS Code and typing in ttyd kept working past 60 s and after a reload. The Coder root in the same window showed the login page.

Cookies the browser holds on the Coder hostname:

| Cookie | Set by | Path | What it is |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `coder_session_token` | the bounce | `/@alice/apps/apps` | the app-only key, 8 h; the only credential the portal gives the browser |
| `coder_signed_app_token` | Coder | the app, e.g. `/@alice/apps/apps/ttyd/` | Coder's own signed ticket for one app, about 1 minute. Coder re-issues it from `coder_session_token` when it expires, which is why the apps keep working (with the token in the URL there is nothing to re-issue it from) |
| `csrf_token` | Coder | `/` | CSRF protection for state-changing requests; not a credential |

### 3.10 Subdomain apps and ports (validated in the local lab, September 27, 2026)

**Where the bounce runs.** Coder serves a subdomain app on its own hostname, for example `code-server--ws--alice.apps.coder.example.com` (Coder leaves the agent out for single-agent workspaces), and a port on `8000--main--ws--alice.apps.coder.example.com` (the agent is required; add `s` after the port for HTTPS, `8443s--…`). A browser only accepts a cookie `Domain` equal to the responding host or a parent of it. So a response from `coder.example.com` could only set `Domain=coder.example.com`, which also reaches the dashboard, and could never narrow it to `apps.coder.example.com`. The bounce therefore runs **on the app hostname itself**: the portal POSTs the code to `https://<app host>/_portal/bounce`, and the Lambda sets the cookie from there.

**Infrastructure.** Everything below except the ALB rule is already needed for Coder's subdomain apps:

| Piece | Setting |
| --- | --- |
| ALB rule | host `*.apps.coder.example.com` + path `/_portal/*` → the same bounce Lambda target group, next to the existing plane-host rule. All other requests on those hosts still go to Coder |
| DNS | `*.apps.coder.example.com` → the ALB |
| Certificate | a SAN for `*.apps.coder.example.com` (a `*.coder.example.com` certificate covers one level only) |
| Middle | `bounce.subdomain_apps: true`, and the plane's `wildcard_url` (e.g. `https://*.apps.coder.example.com`). Optional `subdomain_cookie_name` per plane, in case Coder's cookie name ever differs from the derived one |

The wildcard does not have to sit under the plane hostname, because the cookie is set from the app hostname. The one side effect: a subdomain app can no longer serve its own `/_portal/*` paths, since the ALB sends them to the Lambda.

**What the middle does** (`subdomainTarget` and `portBounceTarget` in `go-middle/bounce.go`):

- The same checks as for path apps: the workspace is the caller's own and running, and the app is healthy. For ports: 1–65535, `http` or `https`, on the workspace's main (or first) agent.
- The hostname label comes from Coder's `subdomain_name` for the app (built as `{port}[s]--{agent}--{ws}--{user}` for ports). It must be a single DNS label of at most 63 characters; a longer one is refused with 400 rather than producing a URL that cannot resolve.
- Cookie name: `coder_subdomain_app_session_token_` plus the first 32 hex characters of sha256 of the wildcard hostname without the port (`*.apps.coder.example.com`). This is the name Coder v2.36.5 uses; Coder accepting the cookie in the lab confirms it.
- `Domain=` the wildcard parent (`apps.coder.example.com`), `Path=/`, the same app-only key and the same 8 h maximum as path apps.
- The code is bound to that app hostname. It cannot be redeemed on another app host or on the plane host.

**Headless checks (`local-subdomain-lab/test-subdomain-bounce.sh`): 33 passed, 0 failed**, plus the two 75-second checks in the first run. Lab: Coder v2.36.5, `--wildcard-access-url '*.coder.127.0.0.1.sslip.io'`, the ALB emulator sending `/_portal/*` on the plane host and on the wildcard hosts to the bounce handler, and the real IAM proof (the lab role `coder-bounce-plane-a`). Template `templates/subdomain-ab`: code-server as a subdomain app, and a web server on port 8000.

| Area | Result |
| --- | --- |
| Access list | code-server marked subdomain and `bounce: true`; `port_bounce: true` |
| Bounce | `bounce_url` on `code-server--sub--alice.coder.127.0.0.1.sslip.io`; 302 to `/`; no token in the redirect |
| Cookie | `coder_subdomain_app_session_token_378c…`, `Domain=coder.127.0.0.1.sslip.io`, `Path=/`, HttpOnly, `SameSite=Lax`, `Max-Age` 28800; no `coder_session_token` set |
| App | VS Code opens with the cookie; without it Coder sends the browser to its auth redirect |
| Ports | port 8000 opens with the cookie from the **VS Code** bounce (the `Domain` covers every app hostname); a bounce on `8000--main--sub--alice…` works too, with the same key |
| After 60 s | VS Code and port 8000 still open at 75 s |
| Dashboard | in this lab the cookie also reaches the dashboard host (the wildcard parent is the plane host), and Coder ignores it there (401). The key itself: `/api/v2/users/me` 404, port-share API 404 |
| Refused | replayed code (403); an app code redeemed on the port's hostname (403, no cookie set); a port code redeemed on the plane host (403, no cookie set); Bob opening Alice's port (404); port 0 (400); protocol `ftp` (400) |

With a wildcard like `*.apps.coder.example.com` the cookie does not reach the dashboard host at all.

**Cookie scope, path apps against subdomain apps.** Same key, different cookie:

| | Path apps | Subdomain apps and ports |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Bounce on | `coder.example.com/_portal/bounce` | `<app>.apps.coder.example.com/_portal/bounce` |
| Cookie | `coder_session_token`, host-only | `coder_subdomain_app_session_token_<hash>`, `Domain=apps.coder.example.com` |
| Path | `/@user/workspace/apps` | `/` |
| Sent to | that workspace's path apps | every host under the wildcard: all the user's subdomain apps and ports, and other users' apps and ports |

Sending it to other users' app hosts gives nothing away: Coder checks on each request whether the key's owner may open that app (sharing level), and every app host is a separate origin whose JavaScript cannot read the HttpOnly cookie. This is how Coder scopes its own subdomain cookie.

**Sharing** (measured with curl in an earlier run of the local subdomain lab, keys placed in the cookie by hand):

| Port `share_level` | Bob, with his own app-only key in the cookie |
| --- | --- |
| `owner` (default) | 404 |
| `authenticated` | 200 |
| `public` | 200, no cookie needed |

The app-only key cannot change sharing (the port-share API answers 404). The owner shares from Coder's UI or CLI, or the middle does it with the owner's full key (`POST`/`DELETE /api/v2/workspaces/{id}/port-share` with `{"agent_name","port","share_level","protocol"}`). Because the cookie covers every app hostname, a link to Alice's `authenticated` port works for Bob as soon as he has bounced once on that plane (today: by opening an app or port of his own workspace; see section 5).

**Reproduce:** `local-subdomain-lab/lab-iam.sh up`, then `DEFAULT_TEMPLATE=subdomain-ab ./bring-up-bounce-lab.sh`, then `./test-subdomain-bounce.sh`; `./lab-iam.sh down` removes the IAM role and user.

