## 6. Reproduce the findings

Against any Coder with a running workspace that has a path app. Use a test user; the key is deleted at the end. Needs `curl` and `jq`.

```bash
CODER=https://coder.example.com
USERNAME=alice
APP="/@alice/notebook/apps/code-server/"
read -rs OWNER_TOKEN   # paste an owner token; not echoed, not in shell history

KEY_JSON=$(curl -s -X POST -H "Coder-Session-Token: $OWNER_TOKEN" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"token_name":"probe-60s","lifetime":3600000000000}' "$CODER/api/v2/users/$USERNAME/keys/tokens")
KEY=$(jq -r .key <<<"$KEY_JSON"); KEY_ID=$(jq -r .id <<<"$KEY_JSON")
JAR=$(mktemp)

# 1.1: token in the URL once, then cookies only
curl -s -o /dev/null -c "$JAR" -w 'first load:  %{http_code}\n' "$CODER$APP?coder_session_token=$KEY"
awk '$6 ~ /coder_/ {print "cookie:", $6, "path:", $3, "expires:", strftime("%T", $5)}' "$JAR"
sleep 30; curl -s -o /dev/null -b "$JAR" -w 'after 30 s:  %{http_code}\n' "$CODER$APP"
sleep 40; curl -s -o /dev/null -b "$JAR" -w 'after 70 s:  %{http_code} %{redirect_url}\n' "$CODER$APP"

# 1.2: a stale cookie beats a valid token in the URL
curl -s -o /dev/null -H 'Cookie: coder_session_token=AbCdEfGhIj-0123456789abcdefghijklm' \
  -w 'stale cookie + valid URL token: %{http_code} %{redirect_url}\n' "$CODER$APP?coder_session_token=$KEY"

curl -s -X DELETE -H "Coder-Session-Token: $OWNER_TOKEN" "$CODER/api/v2/users/$USERNAME/keys/$KEY_ID"
rm -f "$JAR"; unset OWNER_TOKEN KEY KEY_JSON
```

Expected: `first load: 200`; only `coder_signed_app_token` with the app path, expiring 60 s later; `after 30 s: 200`; `after 70 s: 303 …/login?…`; `stale cookie + valid URL token: 303 …/login?…`.

(`curl -b` does not send expired cookies, like a browser. `strftime` needs gawk; drop it to see the raw epoch.)

