## 8. The middle's skeleton: endpoints and sign-in

With the bounce for every app, the middle comes down to about 20 endpoints and **one way of authenticating users: it validates the IdP's JWT**. It has no login form and no session of its own. It drives only two short OIDC redirect flows itself (the `/go/` link and the CLI), and nobody ever signs in to Coder: the middle creates Coder users and keys with the owner token.

### 8.1 Who calls the middle, and how they prove it

| Caller | Credential | Checked by |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Portal (browser JavaScript) | IdP access token, `Authorization: Bearer`. The portal does the OIDC login itself | `requireAuth`: signature against the IdP's JWKS, issuer, audience, email claim |
| `/go/<slug>` link (no portal) | the middle runs OIDC code + PKCE, then hands off to the bounce | `/go/{slug}` → IdP → `/go/callback` |
| CLI and IDEs | the middle runs OIDC code + PKCE (or the portal asks with its Bearer JWT), then returns the Coder URL and a scoped token (section 10). The CLI and IDEs then talk to Coder directly; the middle proxies nothing | `/cli-auth/*`, `/v1/cli-credentials` |
| Bounce (Lambda, or the `bounce-server` microservice) | Lambda: signed `sts:GetCallerIdentity`, bound to this middle, the action and the code. Microservice: projected service-account token, audience = this middle (section 9) | `verifyBounceCaller`: IAM role allowlist per plane; `kind: k8s_sa` entries: issuer + service account, verified via OIDC discovery (section 9.4) |
| Coder (called by the middle) | owner token per plane, server side only | the middle maps email → Coder user (created if missing) and keeps two keys per user and plane: `middle-<plane>` (full, never sent to the browser for apps) and `middle-app-<plane>` (app-only), and mints short-lived scoped tokens for the CLI and IDEs on request (section 10) |

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  U[User] -->|OIDC login| IdP
  U -->|Bearer JWT| M[Middle]
  M -->|owner token: create user, mint keys| C[Coder]
  M -->|one-time code| U
  U -->|form POST code| L[Bounce]
  L -->|caller proof + code| M
  M -->|app-only key + cookie attributes| L
  L -->|Set-Cookie + 302| U
  U -->|cookie| C
```

### 8.2 Endpoints to keep

| Group | Endpoints | Auth |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Identity and catalogue | `GET /v1/me`, `GET /v1/planes`, `GET /v1/templates` | Bearer JWT |
| Workspaces | `GET`/`POST /v1/workspaces`, `DELETE /v1/workspaces/{id}`, `GET /v1/workspaces/{id}/access` | Bearer JWT |
| Logs | `GET /v1/workspaces/{id}/build-logs`, `…/agent-logs` (server-sent events) | JWT, checked in the handler |
| **Open anything in the browser** | `POST /v1/bounce/start`, purposes `app`, `port`, `external-auth` | Bearer JWT |
| GitLab link | `GET`/`DELETE /v1/external-auth/{id}` | Bearer JWT |
| Desktop and CLI (scoped tokens, section 10) | `POST /v1/workspaces/{id}/vscode-desktop`, `GET /v1/cli-credentials` | Bearer JWT |
| Middle-driven sign-in | `GET /go/{slug}`, `GET /go/callback`; `/cli-auth`, `/cli-auth/start`, `/cli-auth/callback`, `POST /cli-auth/mint` | OIDC redirect + PKCE |
| Called by the bounce only | `POST /internal/bounce/redeem`, `POST /internal/bounce/done` | caller proof (`iam` or `sa_token`) |
| Operations | `GET /healthz`, `GET /v1/config/public` | none |

Put `/internal/*` on a separate listener or port that only the bounce can reach (security group, or a NetworkPolicy on Kubernetes), even though each call carries its own proof.

### 8.3 What the bounce makes unnecessary

| To remove | Why |
| --- | --- |
| `GET /v1/workspaces/{id}/coder-app/{slug}`, `/open/{slug}` | open apps with a token in the URL: 60 s (1.1) |
| `POST /v1/app-session`, `handleOpenApp` | older ways of setting an app cookie from the middle's hostname |
| the `middle_session` cookie, `?mt=`, `POST /v1/logout` | the middle's own session; with Bearer only, `resolveAppAuth` shrinks to one case |
| bounce purpose `terminal`, portal `openWebTerminal` | replaced by ttyd as an app (3.7) |
| `/go/` same-host mode | sets the full key with `Path=/`; use the bounce hand-off instead |
| `/v1/tasks/*` | a separate feature with its own task token; keep only if used |

So: **one login** (the IdP), **one token check** in the middle, **one entry point** for opening apps, ports and consent (`/v1/bounce/start`), and **two internal calls** from the bounce.

