## 9. The bounce as a Go microservice (EKS now, OpenShift later)

**Status (September 28, 2026).**
- **Built and tested:** the bouncer, in `spike-middle/bounce-lambda`. It builds, its unit tests pass, the container image builds (`Dockerfile`, 19 MB, distroless, non-root), and it passed a container smoke test.
- **Not yet run end to end** on a cluster.
- **The middle's side (`kind: k8s_sa`) is a design** with tested sample code (9.4); it isn't wired in yet.

The organisation does not want a Lambda in the path. The bounce becomes a small Go service, a Deployment in the cluster that runs Coder. It proves who it is to the middle with a **projected service-account token** instead of IAM. It works the same way on EKS today and on OpenShift later; only what sits in front of it changes. Nothing changes for the browser, the portal, the cookies, Coder, or the middle's redeem logic. Wherever earlier sections say "the Lambda", read "the bounce".

| | Lambda (sections 3–8) | Microservice on EKS | Microservice on OpenShift or on-premises |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Front | ALB rule `/_portal/*` → Lambda target group | the same ALB (AWS Load Balancer Controller `Ingress`): `/_portal/*` → the bounce `Service`, target type `ip` | NGINX ingress, Gateway API `HTTPRoute` or OpenShift `Route`: `/_portal/*` → the bounce `Service` |
| Runtime | Lambda (ALB event) | Deployment `bounce-server` (plain HTTP) | the same image |
| Proof sent to the middle | signed `sts:GetCallerIdentity` | projected SA token, `aud` = the middle's `server_id` | the same |
| The middle verifies with | STS replay + IAM role allowlist | the **EKS cluster's public OIDC discovery** → JWKS; checks `iss`, `aud`, `sub`, `exp`, pod binding | the cluster's issuer: discovery if the middle can reach it, else a published JWKS (`discovery_url`) |
| Allowlist per plane | account + role name | issuer + service account (`system:serviceaccount:<ns>:<name>`) | the same |

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  B[Browser] --> I[ALB on EKS / NGINX or router on OpenShift<br/>coder.example.com, *.apps.coder.example.com]
  I -->|/_portal/*| BP[bounce-server Deployment<br/>projected SA token]
  I -->|everything else| CD[coderd Service]
  BP -->|redeem + SA token| M[Middle]
  M -.->|OIDC discovery + JWKS, cached| OIDC[Cluster issuer<br/>oidc.eks.region.amazonaws.com/id/...]
```

### 9.1 The front: one rule for two hostnames

The rule is the same everywhere. On `coder.example.com` and `*.apps.coder.example.com`, path prefix `/_portal` goes to the bounce `Service` and everything else goes to coderd. The `Host` header must be preserved, which is the default for the ALB, NGINX and the OpenShift router: the bounce and the middle's host check depend on it.

**EKS (AWS Load Balancer Controller).** Join the ALB that already serves Coder with `group.name`, and give the bounce rules a lower `group.order` so they are evaluated before Coder's catch-all rule.

```yaml
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress
metadata:
  name: coder-bounce
  namespace: coder
  annotations:
    alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/group.name: coder            # the same ALB as Coder's Ingress
    alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/group.order: "-10"           # before Coder's "/" rule
    alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/target-type: ip
    alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/listen-ports: '[{"HTTPS":443}]'
    alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/healthcheck-path: /healthz
spec:
  ingressClassName: alb
  rules:
    - host: coder.example.com
      http: &bounce
        paths:
          - path: /_portal
            pathType: Prefix
            backend: { service: { name: bounce, port: { number: 8080 } } }
    - host: "*.apps.coder.example.com"
      http: *bounce
```

If the ALB is managed outside Kubernetes (Terraform), keep today's listener rules and point them at a target group of type `ip` that a `TargetGroupBinding` fills with the bounce pods.

**OpenShift or on-premises (NGINX).** One `Ingress` for both Coder and the bounce:

```yaml
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress
metadata:
  name: coder
  namespace: coder
spec:
  ingressClassName: nginx
  tls:
    - hosts: [coder.example.com, "*.apps.coder.example.com"]
      secretName: coder-tls            # wildcard certificate (cert-manager: DNS-01)
  rules:
    - host: coder.example.com
      http: &paths
        paths:
          - path: /_portal
            pathType: Prefix
            backend: { service: { name: bounce, port: { number: 8080 } } }
          - path: /
            pathType: Prefix
            backend: { service: { name: coder, port: { number: 80 } } }
    - host: "*.apps.coder.example.com"
      http: *paths
```

Notes on the controller:

- **Which NGINX.** The community `ingress-nginx` controller was announced for retirement (end of maintenance March 2026). Use a maintained controller: F5's NGINX Ingress Controller, or a Gateway API implementation (NGINX Gateway Fabric, Envoy Gateway). With Gateway API the same thing is one `HTTPRoute` with both `hostnames` and a `/_portal/` path match; it is the more portable choice between Rancher and OpenShift.
- **OpenShift.** Its default router is HAProxy, not NGINX. Wildcard hosts need `routeAdmission.wildcardPolicy: WildcardsAllowed` on the IngressController and `wildcardPolicy: Subdomain` on the Route; check that a path-based route on a wildcard host behaves as expected on your router version. Alternatively run an NGINX or Gateway API controller next to it for the Coder hostnames.
- **Websockets** go through unchanged (Coder apps, terminals); make sure proxy read timeouts are long enough (e.g. an hour) for idle editor connections.
- The ALB's multi-value header quirk (3.9) only affects Lambda targets. The microservice gets normal HTTP headers, on EKS as well.

### 9.2 The Deployment and its projected token

The pod mounts one token, issued for the middle only, and no Kubernetes API token:

```yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata: { name: bounce, namespace: coder }
automountServiceAccountToken: false
---
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata: { name: bounce, namespace: coder }
spec:
  replicas: 2
  selector: { matchLabels: { app: bounce } }
  template:
    metadata: { labels: { app: bounce } }
    spec:
      serviceAccountName: bounce
      automountServiceAccountToken: false           # no API-server token; only the one below
      securityContext: { runAsNonRoot: true, seccompProfile: { type: RuntimeDefault } }
      containers:
        - name: bounce
          image: registry.example.com/coder/bounce:1   # docker build spike-middle/bounce-lambda
          ports: [{ containerPort: 8080 }]
          env:
            - { name: MIDDLE_URL, value: "https://middle.example.com" }
            - { name: BOUNCE_PROOF, value: sa-token }
            - { name: SA_TOKEN_FILE, value: /var/run/secrets/middle/token }
          readinessProbe: { httpGet: { path: /healthz, port: 8080 } }
          resources: { requests: { cpu: 10m, memory: 16Mi }, limits: { memory: 64Mi } }
          securityContext: { allowPrivilegeEscalation: false, readOnlyRootFilesystem: true, capabilities: { drop: [ALL] } }
          volumeMounts:
            - { name: middle-token, mountPath: /var/run/secrets/middle, readOnly: true }
      volumes:
        - name: middle-token
          projected:
            sources:
              - serviceAccountToken:
                  audience: middle-prod           # = the middle's bounce.server_id
                  expirationSeconds: 600
                  path: token
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata: { name: bounce, namespace: coder }
spec:
  selector: { app: bounce }
  ports: [{ port: 8080, targetPort: 8080 }]
```

The token is a JWT signed by the cluster:
- `iss`: the cluster's issuer. On EKS that's `https://oidc.eks.<region>.amazonaws.com/id/<id>`.
- `aud`: `middle-prod`.
- `sub`: `system:serviceaccount:coder:bounce`.
- It is bound to the pod (claim `kubernetes.io.pod`), valid for 10 minutes, and rotated by the kubelet before it expires.

As with the Lambda role, the bounce holds no long-lived secret.

### 9.3 Go: the bouncer

The code lives in `spike-middle/bounce-lambda` (module `bouncelambda`). The core has no transport and no AWS dependency, and each way of running it is a thin adapter:

| File | Role |
| --- | --- |
| `bounce/handler.go` | the core: `Serve(Request) Response`; redeem at the middle, cookie rules (`safeCookieName`, `safeCookieDomain`, `safeCookiePath`), `/done` |
| `bounce/http.go` | `ServeHTTP`: the microservice adapter (form POST, `Host`, joined `Cookie` headers) |
| `bounce/satoken.go` | `SATokenProver`: reads the projected token on every call |
| `bounce/iamproof/` | the signed `sts:GetCallerIdentity` proof (Lambda, or the microservice with IRSA or Pod Identity) |
| `bounce/alb/` | the ALB → Lambda adapter (kept for existing planes) |
| `cmd/bounce-server/` | the microservice's `main` |
| `Dockerfile` | the multi-stage build to `distroless/static:nonroot` |

The heart of it, shortened from the files above:

```go
package bounce

// Prover returns the JSON field and value the middle checks before it trusts this caller.
type Prover interface {
	Proof(ctx context.Context, action, value string) (field string, proof any, err error)
}

// SATokenProver sends the pod's projected service-account token (audience = middle server_id).
// The kubelet rotates the file, so it is read on every call.
type SATokenProver struct{ Path string }

func (p SATokenProver) Proof(context.Context, string, string) (string, any, error) {
	raw, err := os.ReadFile(p.Path)
	if err != nil {
		return "", nil, err
	}
	return "sa_token", strings.TrimSpace(string(raw)), nil
}

type Handler struct {
	MiddleURL    string
	CookieSecure bool
	Prover       Prover
	HTTP         *http.Client
}

// ServeHTTP: the ingress routes /_portal/* on the plane host and the wildcard app hosts here.
func (h *Handler) ServeHTTP(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
	req := Request{Method: r.Method, Path: r.URL.Path, Host: r.Host,
		Cookie: strings.Join(r.Header.Values("Cookie"), "; ")}
	if r.Method == http.MethodPost {
		r.Body = http.MaxBytesReader(w, r.Body, 16<<10)
		if r.ParseForm() == nil {
			req.Code = r.PostForm.Get("code")
		}
	}
	res := h.Serve(r.Context(), req) // "/done" → done(), otherwise bounce()
	for k, v := range res.Headers {
		w.Header().Set(k, v)
	}
	w.WriteHeader(res.Status)
	io.WriteString(w, res.Body)
}

func (h *Handler) bounce(ctx context.Context, req Request) Response {
	field, proof, err := h.Prover.Proof(ctx, "redeem", req.Code)
	if err != nil {
		return page(http.StatusInternalServerError, "Bounce misconfigured.")
	}
	body, _ := json.Marshal(map[string]any{"code": req.Code, "host": req.Host, field: proof})
	res, err := h.HTTP.Post(h.MiddleURL+"/internal/bounce/redeem", "application/json", bytes.NewReader(body))
	if err != nil {
		return page(http.StatusBadGateway, "Middle unreachable, retry from the portal.")
	}
	defer res.Body.Close()
	if res.StatusCode != http.StatusOK {
		return page(http.StatusForbidden, "This link expired or was already used. Retry from the portal.")
	}
	var out struct {
		SessionToken string `json:"session_token"`
		RedirectPath string `json:"redirect_path"`
		CookieName   string `json:"cookie_name"`
		CookieDomain string `json:"cookie_domain"`
		CookiePath   string `json:"cookie_path"`
		MaxAge       int    `json:"max_age"`
	}
	// ... decode; redirect must be a same-origin path; then the three cookie checks:
	//     name  ∈ {coder_session_token, coder_subdomain_app_session_token_<32 hex>}
	//     domain only for the subdomain cookie, and only a strict parent of req.Host
	//     path   must cover the redirect
	cookie := (&http.Cookie{Name: name, Value: out.SessionToken, Domain: domain, Path: path,
		MaxAge: out.MaxAge, HttpOnly: true, Secure: h.CookieSecure, SameSite: http.SameSiteLaxMode}).String()
	return Response{Status: http.StatusFound, Headers: map[string]string{
		"Location": out.RedirectPath, "Set-Cookie": cookie,
		"Cache-Control": "no-store", "Referrer-Policy": "no-referrer"}}
}
```

And the service (`cmd/bounce-server/main.go`, shortened):

```go
func main() {
	prover := bounce.SATokenProver{Path: env("SA_TOKEN_FILE", "/var/run/secrets/middle/token")}
	if _, _, err := prover.Proof(context.Background(), "", ""); err != nil {
		log.Fatalf("bounce-server: %v", err) // fail fast without a token
	}
	h, err := bounce.FromEnv(prover) // MIDDLE_URL, COOKIE_SECURE
	if err != nil {
		log.Fatal(err)
	}
	mux := http.NewServeMux()
	mux.HandleFunc("GET /healthz", func(w http.ResponseWriter, _ *http.Request) { w.Write([]byte("ok\n")) })
	mux.Handle("POST /_portal/bounce", h)
	mux.Handle("GET /_portal/done", h)
	srv := &http.Server{Addr: ":8080", Handler: accessLog(mux), // method, path, host, status; no query, body or cookies
		ReadHeaderTimeout: 5 * time.Second, ReadTimeout: 10 * time.Second, WriteTimeout: 15 * time.Second,
		MaxHeaderBytes: 32 << 10}
	// SIGTERM → srv.Shutdown (graceful, for rolling updates)
	log.Fatal(srv.ListenAndServe())
}
```

Checked so far: the unit tests (the cookie rules, plus a form POST through `ServeHTTP` against a fake middle that checks the `sa_token` sent and the cookie returned), and a container smoke test. `/healthz` returns 200, a missing code 400, other paths 404, an unreachable middle 502. Without its token file the service refuses to start. The logs contain no code and no cookie.

### 9.4 Go: the middle verifies the token

The allowlist gets a second kind of caller, chosen per entry:

```yaml
bounce:
  server_id: middle-prod                   # = the projected token's audience
  callers:
    - plane: a                             # a plane still on the Lambda
      kind: aws_iam                        # (default)
      account_id: "209561498705"
      role_name: coder-bounce-plane-a
    - plane: b                             # a plane on EKS with bounce-server
      kind: k8s_sa
      issuer: https://oidc.eks.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/id/EXAMPLED539D4633E53DE1B71EXAMPLE
      subject: system:serviceaccount:coder:bounce
      # discovery_url: optional, default <issuer>/.well-known/openid-configuration
```

**On EKS** the issuer is `aws eks describe-cluster --name <cluster> --query cluster.identity.oidc.issuer`. Its discovery document and JWKS are public, so the middle only needs outbound HTTPS to fetch them. It does not need an IAM OIDC provider (that is for IRSA), AWS credentials, or access to the cluster's API.

**On OpenShift or on-premises**, the API server serves the same two documents (`/.well-known/openid-configuration`, `/openid/v1/jwks`). The middle either reaches them, which means allowing unauthenticated discovery (`system:service-account-issuer-discovery`) and trusting the cluster CA, or you publish them somewhere it can reach and set `discovery_url`.

The redeem and done handlers accept `sa_token` next to `iam`. The verifier (compiled and tested: it accepts a valid token and rejects a wrong audience, wrong issuer, another service account, an expired token, one not bound to a pod, a long-lived one, and one with no `exp`):

```go
// saVerifier checks projected service-account tokens from one cluster issuer.
type saVerifier struct {
	issuer   string
	audience string // the middle's bounce.server_id
	kf       keyfunc.Keyfunc
}

// newSAVerifier follows the issuer's OIDC discovery document to its JWKS. keyfunc caches the
// keys and refetches them when a token arrives with an unknown kid (key rotation).
func newSAVerifier(ctx context.Context, issuer, discoveryURL, audience string) (*saVerifier, error) {
	if discoveryURL == "" {
		discoveryURL = strings.TrimRight(issuer, "/") + "/.well-known/openid-configuration"
	}
	ctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(ctx, 10*time.Second)
	defer cancel()
	req, _ := http.NewRequestWithContext(ctx, http.MethodGet, discoveryURL, nil)
	res, err := http.DefaultClient.Do(req)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, fmt.Errorf("oidc discovery: %w", err)
	}
	defer res.Body.Close()
	if res.StatusCode != http.StatusOK {
		return nil, fmt.Errorf("oidc discovery: %s", res.Status)
	}
	var doc struct {
		Issuer  string `json:"issuer"`
		JWKSURI string `json:"jwks_uri"`
	}
	if err := json.NewDecoder(res.Body).Decode(&doc); err != nil {
		return nil, fmt.Errorf("oidc discovery: %w", err)
	}
	if doc.Issuer != issuer {
		return nil, fmt.Errorf("oidc discovery: issuer %q, want %q", doc.Issuer, issuer)
	}
	if !strings.HasPrefix(doc.JWKSURI, "https://") {
		return nil, fmt.Errorf("oidc discovery: jwks_uri %q is not https", doc.JWKSURI)
	}
	kf, err := keyfunc.NewDefaultCtx(context.Background(), []string{doc.JWKSURI})
	if err != nil {
		return nil, fmt.Errorf("jwks: %w", err)
	}
	return &saVerifier{issuer: issuer, audience: audience, kf: kf}, nil
}

type saClaims struct {
	jwt.RegisteredClaims
	K8s struct {
		Namespace string `json:"namespace"`
		Pod       *struct {
			Name string `json:"name"`
			UID  string `json:"uid"`
		} `json:"pod"`
		ServiceAccount struct {
			Name string `json:"name"`
			UID  string `json:"uid"`
		} `json:"serviceaccount"`
	} `json:"kubernetes.io"`
}

const maxSATokenLifetime = time.Hour

// verify checks signature, iss, aud (= server_id), exp/iat, the service account, that the token
// is bound to a pod, and that it is short-lived.
func (v *saVerifier) verify(raw, subject string) (*saClaims, error) {
	var c saClaims
	_, err := jwt.ParseWithClaims(raw, &c, v.kf.Keyfunc,
		jwt.WithValidMethods([]string{"RS256", "ES256"}),
		jwt.WithIssuer(v.issuer),
		jwt.WithAudience(v.audience),
		jwt.WithExpirationRequired(),
		jwt.WithIssuedAt(),
		jwt.WithLeeway(30*time.Second),
	)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	if c.Subject != subject {
		return nil, fmt.Errorf("subject %q not allowed", c.Subject)
	}
	if c.K8s.Pod == nil {
		return nil, errors.New("token is not bound to a pod")
	}
	if c.IssuedAt == nil || c.ExpiresAt.Sub(c.IssuedAt.Time) > maxSATokenLifetime {
		return nil, errors.New("token lifetime too long")
	}
	return &c, nil
}
```

Wiring it into the handlers:
- Build one `saVerifier` per `k8s_sa` entry, lazily, and keep it (keyfunc refreshes the keys).
- In `handleBounceRedeem` and `handleBounceDone`, decode ``SAToken string `json:"sa_token"` ``. If it is set, read `iss` without verifying it (`jwt.NewParser().ParseUnverified`) to pick the entries with that issuer, and call `verify` with each entry's `subject`. The first match gives the plane.
- Otherwise fall back to today's IAM check, which only looks at `aws_iam` entries.
- Everything after that is unchanged: consuming the code, the plane match, the host binding.

Two ways to verify the token; the sample above is the offline one:

| | Offline (OIDC discovery + JWKS) | TokenReview |
| --- | --- | --- |
| How | check the signature against the cluster's published JWKS, then `iss`, `aud` = `server_id`, `exp`, `sub` | the middle asks the cluster's API server (`POST /apis/authentication.k8s.io/v1/tokenreviews`, needs `system:auth-delegator`) |
| Middle needs | the issuer's JWKS (reachable, or copied into config) | network access and credentials to every cluster's API server |
| Catches a deleted pod | no (only the 10-minute expiry) | yes (bound tokens die with the pod) |
| Fits | a middle outside the cluster, several clusters | a middle inside the same cluster |

**Recommendation: offline verification through OIDC discovery.** On EKS it needs nothing but outbound HTTPS from the middle, whereas TokenReview would need an IAM identity and an EKS access entry per cluster. It works the same way for OpenShift, whether the middle stays in AWS or moves next to the plane, and it handles several clusters without giving the middle API access to any of them. The short expiry covers the deleted-pod case well enough.

### 9.5 What changes in the security model

- **The proof is a bearer token, not a per-request signature.** The STS proof is bound to one action and one code (`sha256(action + "\n" + code)` is signed), so a stolen proof cannot redeem another code. A projected token is valid for any redeem during its 10 minutes. Someone who steals it still needs a live one-time code, and codes only travel in the user's own browser POST and are bound to that user and host. Close the remaining gap with transport controls:
  - TLS from the bounce to the middle, so the token never crosses the network in clear;
  - only the bounce reaches the middle's `/internal/*` listener: a NetworkPolicy if the middle runs in the same cluster, otherwise a security group or firewall that admits only the cluster's egress, plus a NetworkPolicy that limits the bounce's own egress to the middle;
  - optionally mTLS between bounce and middle (a service mesh, or cert-manager certificates), which also binds the call to the pod's certificate.
- **Who can obtain a token for `coder/bounce`.** Anyone who can create pods, or `serviceaccounts/token`, in the bounce's namespace can get a token that the middle accepts. Restrict both with RBAC in that namespace. The pod-binding check rejects tokens from a plain `kubectl create token` (no bound pod), but not tokens bound to an existing pod.
- **Everything else stays**: one-time codes bound to user, plane and hostname; the bounce's cookie rules; the app-only key; the middle computing every redirect.

